  "decision": {
    "answer": "NEI",
    "explanation": "The supplied evidence (pmid-19749790) investigates FGFR3 mutations (S249C, Y375C, K652E) in the context of urothelial carcinoma and cellular transformation in vitro. It does not mention Spindle Cell Rhabdomyosarcoma, the EWSR1::TFCP2 fusion, or any diagnostic implications for that specific disease. Therefore, there is no evidence to support, revise, or refute the claim regarding EWSR1::TFCP2 fusion in Spindle Cell Rhabdomyosarcoma.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "document_id": "pmid-19749790",
        "start": 0,
        "end": 595,
        "quote": "We investigated the phenotypic and signaling consequences of three FGFR3 mutations (S249C, Y375C, and K652E) in immortalized normal human urothelial cells (TERT-NHUC) and mouse fibroblasts (NIH-3T3) . . . Interestingly, somatic activating mutations of FGFR3 corresponding to the germline mutations found in skeletal dysplasia syndromes are observed in many urothelial carcinomas (UC) of the bladder, suggesting that in a different cellular context FGFR3 may act as an oncogene. . . . the ability of three mutant forms of FGFR3 that are found in UC (S249C, Y375C, and K652E) to transform NIH-3T3 "
      },
      {
        "document_id": "pmid-19749790",
        "start": 595,
        "end": 805,
        "quote": "cells. In these cells, expression of all FGFR3 mutations resulted in a transformed spindle-like morphology (Figure 1a), higher proliferation rate (p=0.004) (Figure 1b) and colony formation in soft agar (p=0.05)"
      }
    ],
    "unresolved": [
      "No evidence found for EWSR1::TFCP2 fusion in Spindle Cell Rhabdomyosarcoma."
    ],
